<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Merlaud-Ponty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1866">1866</a>–<a href="/years/1915">1915</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as William Ponty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Merlaud-Ponty was a French colonial administrator who served as governor-general of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> from 1908 until his death in 1915. He completed the conquest of the interior, abolished some forms of domestic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and pursued a policy that favoured local chiefs and limited the authority of large chiefdoms. The École normale at Gorée that trained African teachers was named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-merlaud-ponty.jpg" alt="William Merlaud-Ponty" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Merlaud-Ponty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
